# Docker-level configuration for the madmp-dsw stack.
#
# This file is the versioned reference. The one docker compose actually reads is
# .env, which is gitignored because its values differ on every machine:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Since DSW takes its own configuration from the environment, this file is the
# single source of truth for the whole deployment. There is no application.yml
# to keep in step with it.

# --- DSW image version (keep all three DSW images on the same tag) ---
DSW_VERSION=4.31

# --- Dependency versions (a migration may be needed when upgrading) ---
POSTGRES_VERSION=17.5
MINIO_VERSION=RELEASE.2025-05-24T17-08-30Z
# The mc client is a separate image on its own release schedule, so it needs its
# own version. Reusing MINIO_VERSION here asks for a tag that does not exist,
# which is what the upstream bucket script did.
MC_VERSION=RELEASE.2025-05-21T01-59-54Z

# --- Credentials ---
# Written once, here. docker-compose.yml creates the containers with them and
# composes DSW's own connection settings from the same values.
#
# The usernames are fixed on purpose. A username is not a secret, its job is to
# be typed, and a random one would turn `psql -U postgres` into a lookup for no
# gain: what protects these accounts is the password.
#
# The passwords are empty on purpose. Every empty value in this file is one
# scripts/setup.sh generates, differently on every machine. The MinIO one earns
# it: on a Codespace port 9000 is public, so those credentials are all that
# stands between the internet and the object store.
#
# (!!) The Postgres account is created once, at the very first initdb. Changing
# the password later has no effect on an existing database, it takes a
# `docker compose down -v` to start over.
#
# (!!) Setting one by hand: it ends up inside a postgresql:// URI, so a
# character that is special in a URL has to be percent-encoded here, or the
# connection fails in a way that does not name the cause. The generated values
# are hex, which sidesteps this.
POSTGRES_USER=postgres
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
MINIO_ROOT_USER=minio
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=

# --- The three URLs, as the browser reaches them ---
# These are the only values that depend on where the stack runs, because they
# are what the *user's machine* resolves. A Codespace needs its forwarded URLs
# here, since the browser is then on another machine entirely.
#
# S3_URL is the awkward one: DSW has a single setting for MinIO, and it is the
# one handed to the browser inside presigned download URLs, so it has to resolve
# from the containers and from the browser at once. host.docker.internal manages
# both on Docker Desktop only, where the host resolves it to 127.0.0.1 and the
# containers get it through extra_hosts.
API_URL=http://localhost:3000/wizard-api
CLIENT_URL=http://localhost:8080/wizard
S3_URL=http://host.docker.internal:9000

# --- Object storage ---
# Must match the bucket create-bucket.sh creates, or DSW writes into a bucket
# that does not exist.
S3_BUCKET=engine-wizard

# --- Submission webhook (DSW Submit -> dmp-registry) ---
# REGISTRY_* rather than GITHUB_*: compose lets the shell override .env, and
# GITHUB_TOKEN is a name a dev shell often already holds. GitHub also reserves
# the GITHUB_ prefix for Codespaces Secrets.
#
# SUBMISSION_TOKEN is the shared secret DSW sends as "Authorization: Bearer ...".
# The same value must reach DSW — madmp-core's `dsw.publish submission` writes
# it there. Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 24
SUBMISSION_TOKEN=
# Fine-grained PAT: Contents RW + Metadata R on the registry repo.
# Leave empty to run the webhook without GitHub access: it answers /health and
# authenticates DSW, but any real submission fails at the GitHub call.
REGISTRY_TOKEN=
# Account owning the registry repo, and the mono-repo itself.
REGISTRY_OWNER=Pierrott64
REGISTRY_REPO=dmp-registry

# --- DSW signing secrets ---
# Generated by scripts/setup.sh, like the two passwords above. These are the
# values nobody should ever inherit from an example: upstream ships working ones
# in a public repository, so anyone can forge a valid token against an instance
# that keeps them.
#
#   GENERAL_SECRET           openssl rand -hex 16
#   GENERAL_RSA_PRIVATE_KEY  openssl genrsa -traditional 4096
#
# -hex 16 and not more: the secret must be exactly 32 ASCII characters, and the
# server refuses to start on anything else.
#
# -traditional produces the PKCS#1 form starting with "BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY",
# which is what DSW expects, where OpenSSL 3 defaults to PKCS#8.
#
# Setting the key by hand means pasting it between double quotes, keeping its
# line breaks, which docker compose reads correctly:
#
#   GENERAL_RSA_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----
#   MIIJKAIBAAKCAgEAyMMyCZe...
#   -----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----"
#
# (!!) Keep GENERAL_RSA_PRIVATE_KEY last. setup.sh appends the multi-line value
# at the end of the file rather than substituting it in place.
GENERAL_SECRET=
GENERAL_RSA_PRIVATE_KEY=
